// @check
// @hint redundant type annotation: `f` is inferred as `i32`
// @error cannot assign string to i32
// THE OTHER HALF of `elseif-chain-residual.vl`'s soundness argument, and the reason the bare-name
// case needs no invalidation machinery at all.
//
// After `if x is string { return 0 }`, the else arm sees `x` narrowed to `i32` — and that
// narrowing is a shadowing `declare`, so a WRITE inside the else arm is checked against the
// narrowed type, not the declared one. The only way to falsify "x is an i32 below this chain"
// would be to store a `string` into `x` on the else path, and that is exactly what this refuses.
//
// So the post-chain narrowing is not trusting the else arm to behave: the else arm CANNOT
// misbehave in the one way that would matter. (A write the narrowing still admits — `x = 5` —
// is accepted and keeps the fact true, which is why the rule is a type check and not a ban on
// assignment.) Contrast `guard-else-path-alias-write-reject.vl`, where the place is a property
// path, the write is not a direct assignment, and no such argument is available.
function f(x: string | i32): i32 {
  if x is string { return 0 }
  else { x = "clobbered" }
  return x
}
print(f(3))
